\begin{problem}[Fourier inversion on Schwartz space proof dossier]\label{prob:iii11-dossier-11}
Prove the following structural statement and identify the step where its main hypothesis is used: For $f\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R^n)$, $f(x)=\int\widehat f(\xi)e^{2\pi ix\cdot\xi}\,d\xi$ for every $x$.
\end{problem}
\begin{solution}
Proof sketch: the Gaussian transform calculation in III/12 is independent of inversion. Multiply the inverse integrand by exp(-pi epsilon times the squared frequency). Fubini identifies it with convolution by the normalized scaled Gaussian. Approximate-identity recovery gives uniform convergence to f, while integrability of the Schwartz transform gives convergence of the inverse integrals by DCT. The Schwartz seminorm estimates are developed in III/14. This proof also explains why the stated hypothesis is structural rather than cosmetic.
\end{solution}

