\begin{problem}[Green representation proof dossier]\label{prob:iii25-dossier-09}
Prove the following structural statement and identify the step where its main hypothesis is used: For the real Dirichlet Laplacian and smooth data specified above, the classical zero-boundary solution satisfies $u(x)=\int_\Omega G(x,y)f(y)dy$.
\end{problem}
\begin{solution}
Proof sketch: excise a ball around the pole and apply the second Green identity using symmetry of this real Laplace kernel. The outer boundary terms vanish for homogeneous data, the small sphere term tends to u(x), and the volume integral tends to the displayed integral since the kernel singularity is locally integrable. The Green-function existence theorem is used, not proved by this calculation. This proof also explains why the stated hypothesis is structural rather than cosmetic.
\end{solution}

